#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107044 :tutorial/content "Consider the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46a85010d46969ffb7df440efd72bb3fa8d1394b35cc2887ee4567cda15079c5.png)\n\nThe sum of the length of its sides is called the **perimeter**. Here, the perimeter of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AB + BC + AC \\\\\n&= 2 + 4.2 + 3.8 \\\\\n&= 10cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7664d7c3-e47c-57b6-97aa-1131f036852c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2244 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107045 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a quadrilateral $ABCD$ as shown below and it is known that\n\n$$\n{AD} = 6\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\n$$\n{AB} = 2\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\n$$\n{BC} = 4\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nCD=5\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\nWhat is the perimeter of the quadrilateral?\n\n![](images/a7/a74251f1c72eccad42b98e0d3fd9a7e7cd0a60018a6f03bb65ec2abc34eb1c88.png)\n\nRemember that the **perimeter** $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. So, we can simply calculate\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AD + AB + BC + CD \\\\\n&= 6 + 2 + 4 + 5 \\\\\n&= 17m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0428807f-e6a6-5280-81bc-ab454203aebf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2246 :tutorial/title "Perimeters of Polygons"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107046 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the polygons depicted below and find their perimeters.\n\n![](images/69/69ef06a71f13625ad4525a03966f4a4c62ae2b2136b085e97b8dc02a6a6963cc.png)\n\nFrom the picture, we conclude that polygons I, II and III all have a perimeter of $10$ while polygon IV has a perimeter of $8$.\n\nPolygons that have the same perimeter are called **isoperimetric**. So I, II and III are isoperimetric polygons." :tutorial/id #uuid "596db00e-9cf7-51e1-902e-73c6670780eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2247 :tutorial/title "Isoperimetric Polygons"}]}
